        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does solid stain last longer than semi-transparent?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, typically three to five years on a deck floor against two to three for semi-transparent, because the extra pigment blocks more ultraviolet light. The trade is that when solid stain does fail it peels and has to be stripped, while semi-transparent simply thins out and can be washed and recoated.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you put semi-transparent stain over solid stain?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not without stripping the solid stain off completely. A semi-transparent product is designed to soak into wood, and it cannot penetrate an opaque film, so it would sit on the surface and fail quickly. This is why moving from solid back to semi-transparent is the hardest change to make.",
          },
        },
