            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can, because it forms a film on the surface rather than penetrating. Peeling usually follows poor preparation, moisture trapped in the wood, or too many accumulated coats building a film too thick to flex with the boards. Properly applied on well-prepared wood it wears far longer before it lifts.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is semi-solid stain a good compromise?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Often, yes. Semi-solid carries more pigment than semi-transparent so it lasts longer and evens out colour differences, while still penetrating enough that it wears rather than peeling as decisively as a solid coat. It shows the wood's texture without showing much grain.",
