          <a href="#mixing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Mixing Them on One Deck</a>
          <a href="#ten-years" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. The Ten-Year View</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          These are the same product family at different pigment loadings, and the extra pigment does three things at once: it lasts longer, it hides more, and it changes the finish from something that soaks into wood into something that sits on top of it. That third effect is the one worth thinking hardest about.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Semi-transparent</strong> carries light pigment, penetrates the wood, and leaves the grain clearly visible. It lasts two to three years on an exposed deck floor, wears away gradually, and recoats with nothing more than a wash and a fresh coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Solid</strong> carries full pigment, forms an opaque film on the surface, and hides the grain while still showing the wood's texture. It lasts three to five years, covers uneven colour and flaws, and eventually peels rather than wearing, which means stripping.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Real Trade</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You are not choosing between two years and four. You are choosing between an easy recoat forever and a longer interval with a hard reset at the end.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Semi-transparent asks for more frequent but trivial maintenance. Solid asks for less frequent maintenance and one genuinely unpleasant weekend at some point down the line. Both are defensible; they suit different temperaments and different decks.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Side by Side
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Property</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Semi-Transparent</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Solid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grain visibility</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fully visible</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hidden; texture remains</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Life on deck floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2–3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3–5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">How it fails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fades and thins</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cracks and peels</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Recoat process</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean and recoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scrape, spot-prime or strip</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hides flaws</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — shows everything</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, very effectively</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Colour range</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wood tones only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide, including greys and blues</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Reversibility</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy to go more opaque</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard to go back</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Slip resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wood texture retained</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Can be slicker; look for additives</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The colour range row is often the deciding factor for people who care about how the deck looks against the house. Semi-transparent is limited to shades that work with the wood beneath it — cedars, browns, redwoods, greys. Solid can be any colour a paint can be, including matching trim or siding exactly. Our <a href="/deck-stain-colors-guide/">deck stain colours guide</a> covers how to choose within either range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="failure-modes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Each One Fails, Which Is the Whole Argument
          </h2>

          <p>
            A semi-transparent stain lives inside the wood fibres. As it ages, the pigment is consumed by ultraviolet light and the resin gradually erodes, so the finish gets thinner and lighter but never lifts. There is no edge for water to get behind and nothing to flake. When it is spent, the deck simply looks faded and stops beading water.
          </p>

          <p>
            A solid stain lives on top. Its protection comes from being a continuous, opaque layer, and continuity is exactly what it can lose. Water finding a way in at a board end, moisture pushing out from underneath, or a film grown too thick and rigid to flex with the wood all produce the same result: the film cracks, water gets behind it, and it peels away in sheets.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the recoat processes differ so sharply. Renewing a semi-transparent deck means washing it and applying another coat, a Saturday. Renewing a peeling solid deck means scraping everything loose, feathering the edges of what remains, and often stripping the lot — a weekend of genuinely unpleasant work, described in <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">stripping deck stain</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="one-way-door" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The One-Way Door
          </h2>

          <p>
            Moving from semi-transparent to solid is easy. Clean the deck, let it dry, and apply the solid stain — the existing penetrating finish is inside the wood and does not interfere with a film going over the top. People do this routinely as a deck ages and its colour becomes uneven.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moving from solid back to semi-transparent is close to a full restoration. A semi-transparent product works by soaking into wood, and it cannot soak through an opaque film, so every trace of the solid coating has to come off first. That means stripping, sanding, and accepting that residue trapped in the grain may still show. On an older deck it can genuinely be impossible to achieve an even result.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sensible implication is to move up the ladder slowly and only when there is a reason. Start transparent or semi-transparent on a new deck, go semi-solid when colour differences appear, and go solid when the wood has enough history that hiding it is the point. Each step is easy; going backwards is not, which is why the first choice deserves more thought than it usually gets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Deck Suits Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Let the wood's condition make most of the decision.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>New or good-condition wood, consistent colour.</strong> Semi-transparent. There is nothing to hide, and you keep every option open.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Cedar or redwood you want to show off.</strong> Semi-transparent or lighter. Hiding that grain under an opaque coat wastes the timber.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Mixed old and replacement boards.</strong> Solid. Nothing else makes a patched deck read as one surface.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Deep iron stains, old paint marks, permanent damage.</strong> Solid. These show through anything less opaque no matter how well you prepare.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Already coated with a solid product.</strong> Solid, unless you are committed to a full strip.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>You want a specific colour to match the house.</strong> Solid. Semi-transparent can only shift the wood's own tone.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            One important caveat concerns damp decks. A solid film is more vulnerable to moisture pushing out from underneath, so a ground-level deck with poor airflow, or one over damp soil, is a riskier home for a solid coat than an elevated, well-ventilated one. If a deck stays damp underneath, a penetrating finish is the safer choice regardless of appearance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="semi-solid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Semi-Solid, the Underrated Middle Ground
          </h2>

          <p>
            Semi-solid sits between the two and is frequently the most sensible answer for a mid-life deck. It carries considerably more pigment than semi-transparent, so it lasts longer and evens out colour differences between boards, but it still penetrates enough that it thins with wear instead of peeling as decisively as a true solid coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Visually, it shows the wood's texture and a hint of grain without the full pattern. On a deck where some boards have weathered differently, that is often exactly right — enough coverage to unify the surface, not so much that the deck looks painted. Expect roughly three to four years on a floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also preserves more of your options than solid does. Going from semi-solid to solid later is straightforward, and going back down toward semi-transparent, while not easy, is less hopeless than it is from a full opaque film. For a deck that is aging but not yet worn out, this is the rung of the ladder worth considering before jumping to solid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Using Both on the Same Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solid on the railings and semi-transparent on the floor is a common and genuinely sound combination. The reasoning is that the two surfaces face different problems: the floor takes foot traffic, standing water and full sun and benefits from a finish that recoats easily, while railings take less abuse and hold a film far longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also looks deliberate rather than compromised. A solid-stained railing in a colour drawn from the house trim, with a semi-transparent floor showing the wood, is a standard and attractive combination — and it means the railings may need attention only half as often as the deck boards do.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical caution is to keep the products compatible in carrier type and to be careful at the junctions. Do the verticals first, mask or cut in carefully where balusters meet decking, and accept that the transition line needs a steady hand. The application sequence is covered in <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/">how to stain a deck</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ten-years" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Ten-Year View
          </h2>

          <p>
            Over a decade, a semi-transparent deck is stained roughly four times, each time as a wash-and-coat weekend. A solid deck is stained perhaps two or three times, with the later recoats requiring scraping and spot preparation, and at some point a full strip that is a project rather than a job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Total hours across ten years often come out closer than the interval lengths suggest, because the strip absorbs the savings. What genuinely differs is the shape of the commitment: several small predictable jobs versus fewer large unpredictable ones. Most people have a clear preference between those once it is put that way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Material cost follows the same pattern. Solid stains are usually more expensive per gallon and cover less on rough wood, but are bought less often; strippers and the extra preparation products add to the solid column. There is no large financial winner here, which is why the decision should be made on appearance, deck condition, and how you would rather spend your weekends.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Application Differences Worth Knowing</h2>
        <p>
          The two products do not go on the same way. A semi-transparent stain is applied to saturation — one coat on most decks, worked into the grain and with any surplus brushed out or wiped off. A solid stain is applied as a film in two coats, with the full drying time between them, and on bare wood usually wants a primer or a thinned first coat so the porous surface does not pull binder out unevenly.
        </p>
        <p>
          Back-brushing matters for both but for different reasons. On semi-transparent it drives the stain into the wood and prevents pooling. On solid it evens the film thickness and eliminates the roller texture that would otherwise wear through in traffic areas first. Neither product forgives being rolled on and left alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Preparation is stricter for solid stain, because film adhesion is unforgiving of contamination and moisture in a way penetration is not. A semi-transparent stain applied over slightly imperfect preparation gives a slightly imperfect result; a solid stain applied over the same surface peels. The full routine is in <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">prepping a deck for staining</a>, and the coat counts are covered in <a href="/how-many-coats-of-deck-stain/">how many coats of deck stain</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Where Solid Stain Sits Next to Deck Paint</h2>
        <p>
          Solid stain and deck paint are close relatives and often confused. Both are opaque film formers; the difference is that solid stain has a thinner film and a slightly more penetrating formulation, so it retains the wood's surface texture and grips a little better on bare timber. Deck paint builds a thicker, flatter film that hides texture as well as grain and generally lasts longer still.
        </p>
        <p>
          That extra film thickness cuts both ways. It gives paint the longest interval of any deck finish, four to seven years on a floor, but it also makes the eventual failure more dramatic and the removal harder. On horizontal surfaces that take standing water and foot traffic, a thick film has more to lose than a thin one.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you find yourself weighing solid stain against paint rather than against semi-transparent, the deck has effectively already chosen the film-forming route, and the question becomes how far down it to go. That comparison is covered in <a href="/deck-paint-vs-deck-stain/">deck paint versus deck stain</a>, and the products in <a href="/best-deck-paint/">our deck paint testing</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Slip Resistance and Comfort Underfoot</h2>
        <p>
          A film-forming finish on a horizontal surface can be slicker when wet than a penetrating one, because the wood's texture is partially filled rather than left open. On steps in particular, that is worth taking seriously. Many solid deck stains are formulated with slip resistance in mind, and anti-slip additives can be stirred into most products for stairs and high-traffic paths.
        </p>
        <p>
          Heat is the other comfort consideration and it applies to both types equally: dark pigment absorbs more solar energy, and a dark deck in full sun gets genuinely uncomfortable to walk on barefoot. Because solid stain offers the wider colour range, it also offers more opportunity to choose something too dark for the exposure.
        </p>
        <p>
          Mid-tone colours are usually the practical sweet spot on an exposed deck, keeping surface temperatures reasonable while still carrying enough pigment to protect. That is one of the few places where the aesthetic choice and the performance choice point in the same direction, and it is covered further in the <a href="/deck-stain-colors-guide/">colours guide</a>.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Solid for Durability Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The extra year or two is real, but so is the eventual strip. Going solid on a deck in good condition trades an easy recoat forever for a longer interval and one hard weekend later. Make that trade deliberately rather than because the label promises more years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming You Can Go Back Later</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Semi-transparent cannot penetrate an opaque film, so returning to a natural look after a solid coat means stripping everything and accepting that trapped residue may still show. Moving up the opacity ladder is easy; moving down it is close to a restoration.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Semi-Transparent to Hide Flaws</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It shows everything by design. Deep iron staining, mismatched replacement boards and old paint marks all read straight through a light-pigment finish, and no amount of cleaning removes them. If the deck's history needs covering, it needs a semi-solid or solid coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting Solid Stain on a Damp Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A film traps moisture trying to escape from the wood, and that pressure lifts the coating from below. Ground-level decks with poor airflow are the classic case. Where a deck stays damp underneath, a penetrating finish is the safer choice regardless of appearance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Solid Stain Too Often</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each coat adds film thickness, and a thick film loses the flexibility to move with the wood through seasonal expansion, then cracks. A conscientious owner recoating every two years can cause the peeling they were trying to prevent. Recoat on condition, not habit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between solid and semi-transparent deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Semi-transparent carries light pigment, penetrates the wood and leaves the grain visible. Solid carries full pigment, forms a film on the surface and hides the grain while showing texture. The practical difference is that one wears away and the other eventually peels.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does solid stain last longer than semi-transparent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, typically three to five years on a deck floor against two to three, because the extra pigment blocks more UV. The trade is that when solid fails it peels and needs stripping, while semi-transparent simply thins out and can be washed and recoated.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put semi-transparent stain over solid stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not without stripping the solid stain off completely. A semi-transparent product is designed to soak into wood and cannot penetrate an opaque film, so it would sit on the surface and fail quickly. This is the hardest change to make on a deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for an old deck, solid or semi-transparent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Solid, usually. An older deck with weathered boards, replacement boards of different ages and permanent marks looks uneven under a transparent finish, and solid stain covers all of it uniformly. Semi-transparent suits wood still in good, consistent condition.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does solid deck stain peel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, because it forms a film rather than penetrating. Peeling usually follows poor preparation, moisture trapped in the wood, or too many accumulated coats building a film too thick to flex. Properly applied on well-prepared wood it wears far longer before lifting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is semi-solid stain a good compromise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes. It carries more pigment than semi-transparent so it lasts longer and evens out colour differences, while still penetrating enough to wear rather than peel decisively. It shows the wood's texture without showing much grain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which stain is better for a deck floor versus railings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many decks use solid on railings and semi-transparent on the floor, which is sound. Verticals take less wear and less standing water, so a film survives well there, while the floor benefits from a penetrating product that recoats without stripping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does solid stain hide wood grain completely?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It hides the grain pattern and colour variation but not the surface texture, so the deck still reads as wood rather than as painted board. That is the main visual difference between solid stain and deck paint, which builds a thicker, flatter film.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

