        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Semi-transparent penetrates, shows the grain, lasts two to three years and wears away — so every recoat is a wash and a coat. Solid forms a film, hides the grain and any flaws beneath it, lasts three to five years, and eventually peels, which means stripping.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Let the wood decide. Good, consistent boards with visible grain worth showing call for semi-transparent. Mixed replacement boards, permanent staining or an existing solid coat call for solid. Semi-solid genuinely splits the difference and is the right answer for a lot of mid-life decks.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Above all, remember which direction is easy. Going more opaque later is simple; going back to a natural look after a solid coat means stripping everything and hoping the residue does not show. Move up the ladder only when the deck gives you a reason to.
          </p>
